# 6. VOLATILITY INTELLIGENCE ENGINE

Use volatility to adjust the sensitivity of existing strategies.

## 6.1 Low Volatility

When volatility is low:

- Recognize compression.
- Reduce unnecessary momentum triggers.
- Watch for relevant breakout levels.
- Evaluate range-edge opportunities.
- Avoid interpreting tiny absolute movement as strong displacement.

## 6.2 Normal Volatility

Use Balanced settings.

Allow normal liquidity, SMC, candle psychology and continuation setups.

## 6.3 Elevated Volatility

- Evaluate stronger displacement.
- Use volatility-normalized wick and zone tolerances.
- Avoid incorrectly identifying every large candle as a new signal.
- Check whether the setup is already overextended.
- Consider spread and execution limitations when data is available.

## 6.4 Extreme Volatility

- Flag the environment as high risk.
- Apply hard execution safeguards.
- Avoid chasing highly extended movement.
- Permit only eligible setups with acceptable execution assumptions.
- Return WAIT if data quality or risk conditions are unsuitable.

Do not assume every period of high volatility is profitable.

### Important

Never dynamically change stop loss or target levels for a previously published setup solely because an automatic volatility setting changes later.

Existing signals should preserve their decision-time configuration snapshot.

